WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.080
<v James Cridland>Coming up, iHeart's Podcasts for Sale or not?

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.200
<v Announcer>With James Cridland and Sam Sethi.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:12.720
<v Announcer>This is the Podnews Weekly Review with Buzz with Buzzsprout.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.000
<v Announcer>Start podcasting, he he keep podcasting.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:26.879
<v Sam Sethi>James, so let's start off with what is allegedly a big story coming out of Bloomberg that iHeart is putting up its podcast business for sale.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:27.839
<v Sam Sethi>Tell me more.

00:00:28.239 --> 00:00:28.960
<v James Cridland>Or is it?

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:35.679
<v James Cridland>So according to Bloomberg, it cites apparent discussions with an investment bank.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.640
<v James Cridland>So, and they're basically talking about um potentially selling it off.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.000
<v James Cridland>Bit of a weird thing to do, given that it's the bit of iHeart which is growing.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:53.039
<v James Cridland>But in response, iHeart has said in a statement that it has no plans to sell our podcasting business.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:04.400
<v James Cridland>So I would remind you that Bloomberg also reported earlier on this year that uh iHeart was going to merge with SiriusXM, and that weirdly didn't happen either.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.719
<v James Cridland>So I'm not quite sure what's going on there.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.319
<v James Cridland>I guess so.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:19.680
<v James Cridland>A couple of people have said a iHeart's shares have had a dreadful time since Monday when they released their uh their uh financial uh numbers for the quarter.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.480
<v James Cridland>This has actually stopped that from going down, so that's kind of one thing.

00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:35.760
<v James Cridland>The other thing is that, well, iHeart is actually planning to split out its podcast business from its audio group so that investors can actually see how well it's doing.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:42.159
<v James Cridland>So that doesn't seem to me to be the sort of thing that any company that would want to sell would do.

00:01:42.319 --> 00:01:44.079
<v James Cridland>So not so sure, to be honest.

00:01:44.239 --> 00:01:45.200
<v James Cridland>What do you think, Sam?

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.760
<v James Cridland>Do you think iHeart selling or or not?

00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:50.640
<v Sam Sethi>I think there's no smoke without fire.

00:01:50.799 --> 00:02:00.719
<v Sam Sethi>I think if you keep hearing rumors in the market often and frequently, then somebody in the know is getting a bit of a message and saying, Is it now?

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.400
<v Sam Sethi>Can I get the exclusive?

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:03.359
<v Sam Sethi>Yes.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.879
<v James Cridland>Yes, and somebody else has said has said where the smoke dot dot dot.

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:09.520
<v James Cridland>Yes.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:14.000
<v James Cridland>And I think, yeah, I think that that's probably that probably makes a bit of sense.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.439
<v James Cridland>It may just have been a rumour just to get the stock price up.

00:02:17.759 --> 00:02:23.520
<v James Cridland>Because they've fallen by the stock price has fallen by two-thirds, two-thirds of its value in a week.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.120
<v James Cridland>So it might just be that.

00:02:25.759 --> 00:02:30.800
<v Sam Sethi>Microsoft in the early days, their share price will remain pretty flat under Barmer.

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:49.280
<v Sam Sethi>But when MSN was in the ascendancy, there was a lot of talk about running a side-by-side running track, a secondary share in effect, so that the value of MSN would be seen to the market, which would boost the parent share.

00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:51.280
<v Sam Sethi>And I wonder whether they're trying to do the same thing.

00:02:51.439 --> 00:02:53.759
<v Sam Sethi>Look, we've got a really successful Cash Cow.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:01.439
<v Sam Sethi>Uh well, we in the Boston Consulting Matrix, the Cash Cow, the radio, is now going into decline, it's becoming a slight dog.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:09.199
<v Sam Sethi>But we've got a rising star of this thing called Podcast, and you can't really see it when we aggregate all the numbers, but it is really good.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:17.599
<v Sam Sethi>So let's strip it out, create a separate track that you can see, and maybe you'll give us some benefit in the parent share eventually.

00:03:17.759 --> 00:03:24.879
<v Sam Sethi>Um, I think the other thing though that is quite worrying, they carry 4.9 billion in long-term borrowings.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.919
<v Sam Sethi>Again, maybe that was the rumour that they're going to sell off because they've got to pay back.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:30.479
<v Sam Sethi>I don't know.

00:03:30.719 --> 00:03:44.000
<v James Cridland>Matt Deegan, friend of the show, says that iHeart still trying to recover from the crazy clear channel financing of the past, which I would certainly agree with, but potentially selling the key growing product of the business, he says, is mad.

00:03:44.159 --> 00:03:49.840
<v James Cridland>It also means the potential owner loses out on the app stroke radio cross promotion.

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:52.319
<v James Cridland>He also says he's not sure who's big enough to buy it.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.680
<v James Cridland>I mean, Sirius XM might be.

00:03:53.759 --> 00:03:58.639
<v James Cridland>They've got a brand new CEO starting in January, so who knows what might happen there.

00:03:58.719 --> 00:04:00.319
<v James Cridland>But I think I I would agree.

00:04:00.479 --> 00:04:04.560
<v James Cridland>I mean, why would you sell the best performing bit of your business?

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:08.159
<v James Cridland>Now, it's not the bit of the business that brings in the most amount of money.

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:14.879
<v James Cridland>That's that that's still the legacy radio stations, but those are in decline, as you so rightly say.

00:04:15.199 --> 00:04:19.600
<v James Cridland>Podcasting is, you know, I mean, they were up 24.4% year on year.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:20.879
<v James Cridland>I uh, you know.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:31.759
<v James Cridland>I have to say, I think that this is and that this is a weird story that's probably there as a bit of a rumour to push up the share price, and that might be it.

00:04:32.079 --> 00:04:35.279
<v James Cridland>But uh yeah, I'm not so sure in terms of that.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:42.879
<v Sam Sethi>I wanted to include this next story, James, because of your radio futurologist hat that you can ply.

00:04:44.079 --> 00:04:51.920
<v Sam Sethi>The UK Rajar released some figures around radio uh and radio listening across age groups and across demographics.

00:04:52.079 --> 00:04:56.639
<v Sam Sethi>And a friend of the show, Adam Bowie, took a closer look at the numbers as well.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:10.240
<v Sam Sethi>One of the things that stood out for me was a chart that Adam produced which showed the percentage of radio listening compared to on-demand music, podcasts, catch-up radio, audiobooks, etc.

00:05:10.959 --> 00:05:29.439
<v Sam Sethi>So it shows fundamentally radio still the dominant means of listening to music or listening to information compared to podcasting, which was micro-small, much smaller than I thought, you know, sub-10% in most categories.

00:05:29.839 --> 00:05:32.079
<v Sam Sethi>It goes back to the story with iHeart.
